\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{What the gluing theorem establishes about descent}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{The [presheaf of Gaussian laws](fdip-0064) has an explicit domain,
restriction maps and overlap compatibility. On the finite-subset
poset with covers given by unions, it is not a sheaf: the [two-singleton
cover](fdip-006A) has non-unique extensions, and the [four-cycle edge cover](fdip-0067)
has compatible data with no extension. These are separate failures
of uniqueness and existence.}

\p{The [chordal theorem](fdip-0068) gives a precise replacement on a
specified clique cover. It chooses the unique extension satisfying
that graph's Markov condition and supplies its density and precision.
A different graph or a different collection of local laws is a
different problem. This does not turn fixed-graph Markov laws into
a presheaf closed under every marginal restriction; Schur elimination
can introduce precision edges.}

\p{The earlier [factorization comparison](fdip-004Q) separates scalar
factorization, functorial factorization, localization and descent.
The present result adds a concrete finite local-to-global theorem
for laws, but no descent data for the objects and arrows of the
[background groupoid](fdip-000X). A stack would require a specified
site, a category fibred over it, sheaves of morphisms and effective
object descent
\citet{Definition 8.4.1, Tag 0268}{stacks2017stacks}.
No such stack assertion follows from the density gluing formula;
stack-level gluing of background objects remains an additional
question.}
